 "drivingQuestion": "Why mention four if five is permitted?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rav Ḥisda distinguishes sandal sizes",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.60b.17",
   "daf": "60b",
   "sentenceId": 16,
   "he": "אַרְבַּע אוֹ חָמֵשׁ מוּתָּר. הַשְׁתָּא חָמֵשׁ שְׁרֵי, אַרְבַּע מִיבַּעְיָא?! אָמַר רַב חִסְדָּא: אַרְבַּע מִסַּנְדָּל קָטָן, וְחָמֵשׁ מִסַּנְדָּל גָּדוֹל.",
   "en": "The Gemara continues its detailed analysis of the Tosefta: It was taught that if most of the nails in the sandal came out and only «four or five» nails remain, it «is permitted» to go out wearing it. The Gemara asks: «Now,» if it was mentioned that when «five» nails remain, going out «is permitted, is it necessary» to mention «four? Rav Ḥisda said» that the Tosefta means: If «four» nails remain «from» the nails in «a small sandal, and» if «five» nails remain «from» the nails in «a large sandal,» going out is permitted."
  }
 ],
